<!-- ===================== MASTER PROMPT ===================== -->
<h2>MASTER PROMPT (copy this to the agent first)</h2>
<div class="prompt">You are an academic technical writer. Write a complete Final Year Project (FYP) report in formal academic English (British/American spelling consistent; prefer British if unsure).

Use ONLY the facts in the attached "FYP Report Generation Brief". Do not invent clinical claims, hospital trials, or metric numbers that are not in the brief. Where numbers are missing, write placeholders like [INSERT FROM training/results/comparison.md] and explain what should be inserted.

Tone: third person, past tense for work done ("was developed", "were trained"), present tense for system behaviour. No hype. Always state that the system is a research demonstration on public datasets and NOT a clinical diagnostic device.

Structure: follow the exact heading map in the brief (Front Matter → Chapters 1–6 → Back Matter / Appendices). Expand every heading into proper paragraphs (not bullet-only chapters). Include tables where specified. Suggest figure captions where screenshots/diagrams are needed.

Length target: substantial FYP report (roughly equivalent to 50–70 pages of content if printed). Literature Review should cite real well-known papers/datasets (CheXNet, ImageNet, DenseNet, EfficientNet, RSNA/Kaggle pneumonia, TB datasets) with IEEE-style references; mark uncertain citations as [VERIFY].

At the end of each chapter, write a short Summary subsection if the map requires it.

Output format: clean Word-ready headings (Heading 1/2/3), numbered chapters, tables with captions, and a References list. Leave [FILL IN] for student/university identity pages.</div>

<!-- ===================== PROJECT FACT SHEET ===================== -->
<h2>PROJECT FACT SHEET (single source of truth)</h2>

<h3>What the system does now</h3>
<p>A Flask web application that accepts a chest X-ray upload and runs <strong>all available trained models</strong> over the <strong>same four labels</strong>, then shows each model’s probability distribution, an <strong>ensemble verdict</strong> (soft voting / mean probability), and whether the models <strong>agree</strong>.</p>

<p><strong>Locked label space (order matters):</strong> Normal · Tuberculosis · COVID-19 · Pneumonia</p>

<table>
<tr><th>Architecture</th><th>Initialisation</th><th>Input</th><th>Preprocess</th><th>Weights file</th></tr>
<tr><td>EfficientNetB3</td><td>ImageNet transfer</td><td>256×256</td><td>EfficientNet preprocess_input</td><td>weights/efficientnetb3_4class.keras</td></tr>
<tr><td>DenseNet121</td><td>ImageNet transfer</td><td>224×224</td><td>DenseNet preprocess_input</td><td>weights/densenet121_4class.keras</td></tr>
<tr><td>Custom CNN</td><td>From scratch</td><td>150×150</td><td>/255 rescale</td><td>weights/cnn_4class.keras</td></tr>
</table>

<h3>Core methodological claims (defendable)</h3>
<ul>
<li>Three architectures trained on <strong>one shared manifest</strong> (same data, same patient-grouped splits, same seed, same epoch budget) so comparison is fair.</li>
<li>Transfer models share an <strong>identical classification head</strong>: GAP → Dropout(0.5) → Dense(256, ReLU) → Dropout(0.3) → Dense(4, Softmax).</li>
<li>Fine-tune capacity matched by <strong>fraction of weights</strong> (~43%): DenseNet from layer 300; EfficientNet from layer 340. BatchNorm stays frozen in fine-tune.</li>
<li>No warm-start from old single-disease weights (would bias the benchmark).</li>
<li>Splits: <strong>patient-grouped</strong>; duplicates removed by <strong>content hash</strong>; Normal &amp; Pneumonia drawn from multiple sources to reduce scanner shortcutting.</li>
<li>Class imbalance: COVID small (~130 after dedupe); others capped (~400); residual imbalance handled by <strong>inverse-frequency class weights</strong>.</li>
<li>Evaluation emphasises <strong>per-class precision/recall/F1</strong> and <strong>balanced accuracy</strong>, not headline accuracy alone; plus leakage_probe for source shortcutting.</li>
<li>Deployment: 3-stage chest X-ray gate; “Analyse anyway” override; train/serve resize aligned (bilinear).</li>
</ul>

<h3>Historical prior work (separate from 4-class results)</h3>
<p>Earlier single-disease models: TB EfficientNetB3 (~95.63% test binary), COVID DenseNet121 (~85–95% expected 3-class), Pneumonia custom CNN (test accuracy 92%; Normal P/R/F1 0.90/0.87/0.88; Pneumonia 0.92/0.94/0.93 on 624 images). TB architecture benchmark table (VGG19 best at 99.37% test) is <strong>historical only</strong> and not comparable to 4-class metrics.</p>

<h3>Implementation status note for honesty</h3>
<p>Code for unified training, evaluation, leakage probe, and multi-model frontend is complete. Final 4-class numeric tables come from <code>training/evaluate.py</code> → <code>training/results/comparison.md</code> after training. Portable inference bundle can ship with pre-trained <code>*_4class.keras</code> weights. Report must distinguish “system designed and implemented” vs “insert final measured metrics after evaluation run”.</p>

<h3>Tech stack</h3>
<p>Python 3.9+/3.11 recommended for TF 2.16; TensorFlow/Keras; OpenCV; Flask; NumPy; scikit-learn (evaluation); Matplotlib/Seaborn (figures). Key folders: <code>training/</code>, <code>Frontend/</code>, <code>weights/</code>, source datasets under TB / DenseNet121/Covid19-dataset / pneumonia_detection/data.</p>

<!-- ===================== FRONT MATTER ===================== -->
