<p>Must cover: (1) problem of multi-disease CXR screening and incomparable single-disease models; (2) unified 4-class task Normal/TB/COVID/Pneumonia; (3) three architectures EfficientNetB3, DenseNet121, Custom CNN on shared manifest; (4) patient-grouped splits, dedupe, class weights; (5) Flask app with multi-model + soft-voting ensemble + X-ray gate; (6) evaluation via per-class metrics and leakage probe; (7) research-only disclaimer. Insert final accuracy/F1 only if available from comparison.md; else omit exact claims.</p>

<h3>Acknowledgement</h3>
<p>Thank supervisor, department, family/friends, and any open-data providers (Kaggle TB, pneumonia; COVID public set). Keep sincere and short (½–1 page).</p>

<h3>Project in Brief</h3>
<p>One-page executive summary: goal, 4 classes, 3 models, unified training pipeline, web demo, main contributions (fair architecture benchmark + ensemble CAD demo + honest evaluation design), tools (Python, TF, Flask), and limitation (public small data; not clinical device).</p>

<h3>Table of Contents / List of Figures / List of Tables</h3>
<p>Auto-generate from Word styles. Suggested figures: system architecture; data merge flow; model head diagram; training two-phase flowchart; X-ray gate stages; UI screenshots (upload, results, rejection); confusion matrices; comparison bar chart. Suggested tables: datasets; hyperparameters; architecture comparison; metrics tables; SDG mapping.</p>

<!-- ===================== CHAPTER 1 ===================== -->
<h2>CHAPTER 1 — INTRODUCTION</h2>

<h3>Motivation</h3>
<p>Write about global burden of TB, COVID-19, and pneumonia; role of chest radiography as widely available screening tool; shortage of radiologists in low-resource settings; promise of CNNs/transfer learning; need for tools that handle <strong>multiple</strong> respiratory conditions in one workflow rather than separate siloed apps. Motivate fair comparison of architectures so engineering choices are evidence-based.</p>

<h3>Problem Statement</h3>
<p>State clearly: Existing student/demo systems often train separate models for separate diseases on incompatible label spaces, preventing fair comparison and preventing a single upload from receiving a multi-disease assessment. Data leakage (patient overlap, duplicates) and train–serve preprocessing mismatch further undermine reported accuracy. <strong>Problem:</strong> Design and implement a reproducible multi-architecture system that classifies chest X-rays into Normal, Tuberculosis, COVID-19, or Pneumonia under shared data/splits, with transparent metrics and a usable web interface including input validation and ensemble aggregation.</p>

<h3>Aim and Objectives</h3>
<p><strong>Aim:</strong> To develop a research-grade chest X-ray multi-disease classification system that benchmarks three deep learning architectures on a unified four-class dataset and deploys them through an ensemble-capable web application.</p>
<p><strong>Objectives:</strong></p>
<ol>
<li>Merge three public CXR sources into a unified 4-class manifest with deduplication and patient-grouped splits.</li>
<li>Implement EfficientNetB3, DenseNet121, and a custom CNN with matched training protocol and shared head (transfer models).</li>
<li>Train and evaluate with per-class precision, recall, F1, balanced accuracy, confusion matrices, and a source-leakage probe.</li>
<li>Build a Flask application that runs all models per upload, computes soft-voting ensemble, and applies a multi-stage chest X-ray gate.</li>
<li>Document methodology, limitations, and reproducibility (manifest, seeds, scripts).</li>
</ol>

<h3>Organization of the Report</h3>
<p>One short paragraph per remaining chapter: Ch.2 literature; Ch.3 methodology; Ch.4 system design; Ch.5 results/discussion; Ch.6 future/conclusion; appendices (data, models, SDGs).</p>

<!-- ===================== CHAPTER 2 ===================== -->
<h2>CHAPTER 2 — LITERATURE REVIEW</h2>
<p>Agent should write a proper review (~8–15 pages equivalent) using the subsection map. Tie each subsection back to this project’s choices.</p>

<h3>Deep Learning in Medical Imaging</h3>
<p>CNNs for radiology; CAD history; shift from handcrafted features to end-to-end learning; importance of large labelled corpora and transfer learning when medical data are scarce.</p>

<h3>Foundational CNN Architectures</h3>
<p>Brief evolution: AlexNet → VGG → ResNet → DenseNet (feature reuse) → EfficientNet (compound scaling). Explain why this project selected DenseNet121, EfficientNetB3, and a compact custom CNN as comparison axes (transfer vs scratch; dense connectivity vs efficient scaling vs lightweight custom).</p>

<h3>Large-Scale Chest X-Ray Benchmarks</h3>
<p>Discuss CheXpert, ChestX-ray14 / CheXNet-style work, NIH datasets; multi-label vs multi-class settings; note that this project uses a <strong>4-class single-label</strong> formulation for clarity and fair softmax comparison.</p>

<h3>COVID-19 Detection from Chest X-Rays</h3>
<p>Pandemic-era surge in CXR AI; risks of spurious shortcuts (hospital tokens, dataset bias); small COVID sets; why per-class recall and leakage tests matter. Link to this project’s COVID as minority class and Viral Pneumonia→Pneumonia merge.</p>

<h3>Tuberculosis Detection</h3>
<p>TB screening with CXR in endemic regions; prior transfer-learning studies; mention this project’s earlier binary TB benchmark (historical) and current inclusion of TB as one of four unified classes.</p>

<h3>Pneumonia Detection</h3>
<p>Kaggle RSNA/Mooney pneumonia dataset prevalence in student projects; binary Normal/Pneumonia CNNs; class imbalance; this project’s prior 92% CNN results as historical baseline, now extended to 4-class head.</p>

<h3>Transfer Learning</h3>
<p>ImageNet pretraining; freeze-then-fine-tune; learning-rate schedule; catastrophic forgetting; this project’s two-phase protocol and matched unfreeze fractions.</p>

<h3>Data Augmentation and Regularization</h3>
<p>Geometric augmentation for CXR; Dropout; BatchNorm; class weights vs oversampling; EarlyStopping / ReduceLROnPlateau / Checkpointing as used here.</p>

<h3>Multi-Disease Detection Frameworks</h3>
<p>Survey multi-label and multi-task CXR systems; contrast with this project’s mutually exclusive 4-class softmax + multi-model ensemble at inference (architecture consensus rather than multi-label heads).</p>

<h3>Deployment and Clinical Translation</h3>
<p>From notebook to web/API; DICOM/PACS gaps; regulatory/ethical limits; this project’s Flask demo scope and explicit non-diagnostic disclaimer.</p>

<h3>Explainability and Trust</h3>
<p>Grad-CAM, saliency; clinician trust; note as <strong>gap / future work</strong> for this FYP (not implemented).</p>

<h3>Gaps in Existing Research</h3>
<p>List gaps this project addresses: (1) incomparable single-disease student models; (2) leakage-unaware splits; (3) accuracy-only reporting; (4) train–serve skew; (5) weak input validation for non-X-ray uploads; (6) lack of ensemble + disagreement surfacing.</p>

<h3>Summary</h3>
<p>Condense: literature supports CNNs + transfer learning for CXR; careful data hygiene and metrics are essential; this work responds with a unified 4-class multi-architecture benchmark and deployable ensemble app.</p>

<!-- ===================== CHAPTER 3 ===================== -->
<h2>CHAPTER 3 — METHODOLOGY</h2>
<p>(Align with existing FYP_Chapter3_Methodology.doc; expand to match these exact subheads.)</p>

<h3>From Single-Disease Models to a Unified Benchmark</h3>
<p>Narrate the evolution: three siloed models → cannot compare → convert to shared 4-class label space → all architectures retrain from common starting rules (ImageNet / scratch) on one manifest.</p>

<h3>Data Collection and Unified Manifest</h3>
<p>Describe <code>training/build_manifest.py</code> producing <code>training/manifest.csv</code> with path, class, patient id, source, split. Constraint: existing public datasets only.</p>

<h3>Source Datasets</h3>
<table>
<tr><th>Dataset</th><th>Path</th><th>Supplies</th></tr>
<tr><td>TB Chest X-Ray (Kaggle)</td><td>TB_Chest_Radiography_Database/</td><td>Tuberculosis, Normal</td></tr>
<tr><td>Covid19-dataset</td><td>DenseNet121/Covid19-dataset/</td><td>COVID-19, Normal, Pneumonia</td></tr>
<tr><td>Chest X-Ray Pneumonia (Kaggle)</td><td>pneumonia_detection/data/chest_xray/</td><td>Pneumonia, Normal</td></tr>
</table>
<p>Viral Pneumonia merged into Pneumonia. COVID caps scale of other classes.</p>

<h3>Design Rules for Honest Splits</h3>
<ol>
<li>Content-hash deduplication</li>
<li>Patient-grouped train/val/test</li>
<li>Fully qualified patient IDs (avoid filename collisions like 0101.jpeg)</li>
<li>Multi-source Normal &amp; Pneumonia</li>
<li>Class-size capping + class weights</li>
<li>Inspect class×source table before training</li>
</ol>

<h3>Model Architectures and Shared Head</h3>
<p>Detail EfficientNetB3, DenseNet121, Custom CNN 5-block stack; shared head; softmax+categorical CE; FINETUNE_FROM densenet=300, efficientnet=340; BatchNorm frozen in phase 2; CNN scratch baseline.</p>

<h3>Training Protocol</h3>
<p>Shared seed 1337; batch 32; Phase1 LR 1e-4 frozen; Phase2 LR 1e-5 partial unfreeze; Adam; callbacks; outputs weights/*_4class.keras and training/runs/*.json; test never used in training.</p>

<h3>Evaluation Methodology</h3>
<p><code>evaluate.py</code>: accuracy, balanced accuracy, per-class P/R/F1, confusion matrices, ensemble, comparison.md. <code>leakage_probe.py</code>: predict source vs majority baseline. Why COVID recall is the critical weak point.</p>

<h3>Application Integration Workflow</h3>
<p>Upload → gate → predict_all → soft ensemble → UI. Weights must be 4-wide; legacy .h5 rejected.</p>

<h3>Summary</h3>
<p>Methodology authenticates fair comparison + honest metrics + deployable inference path.</p>

<!-- ===================== CHAPTER 4 ===================== -->
<h2>CHAPTER 4 — SYSTEM DESIGN</h2>

<h3>System Architecture</h3>
<p>Describe layered view: Presentation (HTML/CSS/Jinja) → Application (Flask routes) → Inference service (predictors: lazy load, lock, ensemble) → Model artefacts (weights/) → Data pipeline (training offline). Draw ASCII/Mermaid: User → Flask → Gate → EfficientNet/DenseNet/CNN → Ensemble → Result page.</p>

<h3>Functional Requirements</h3>
<ul>
<li>FR1: Accept PNG/JPG/JPEG uploads (max 16 MB)</li>
<li>FR2: Validate likely chest radiograph; allow forced override</li>
<li>FR3: Run every available 4-class model on the same image</li>
<li>FR4: Display per-class probabilities per model</li>
<li>FR5: Compute and display soft-voting ensemble and agreement</li>
<li>FR6: Show clear errors if weights missing / wrong output width</li>
<li>FR7: Offline training scripts to rebuild weights from manifest</li>
</ul>

<h3>Non-Functional Requirements</h3>
<ul>
<li>NFR1: Reproducibility (seed, committed manifest policy)</li>
<li>NFR2: Usability (single upload, no model picker required)</li>
<li>NFR3: Robustness to non-X-ray uploads</li>
<li>NFR4: Train/serve preprocessing consistency</li>
<li>NFR5: Maintainability (shared CLASSES in config/common)</li>
<li>NFR6: Explicit non-clinical safety messaging</li>
</ul>

<h3>Tools and Technologies Used</h3>
<p>Python; TensorFlow/Keras; OpenCV; Flask/Werkzeug; NumPy; HTML/CSS; Git; optional Jupyter for historical notebooks. Hardware note: training preferably on capable machine/Mac; inference on CPU possible but slower.</p>

<h3>Chest X-Ray Validation Gate</h3>
<p>Three stages: (1) monochrome/tint; (2) radiograph envelope features with one violation forgiven; (3) anatomy veto (spine contrast + symmetry). Measured on 351 real vs 106 non-X-ray: 100% real accepted; ~77% non-X-ray rejected (old gate ~21%). Rejection reasons shown; Analyse anyway path secured against path traversal.</p>

<h3>Application Flow and User Interface</h3>
<p>Home page lists model readiness; upload form; results show probability bars, class blurbs, ensemble, agreement, forced-analysis warning if used. Suggest screenshots as Figure captions.</p>

<h3>Train/Serve Consistency</h3>
<p>Explain bilinear resize aligned with training decode; same preprocess_input /rescale; CLASSES order shared; refusal of non-4-class weights.</p>

<h3>Summary</h3>
<p>System design realises methodology as a usable, safer demo with multi-model consensus.</p>

